\citation{Baumann_2022}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces In simple inflationary models, the Universe at early times is dominated by the potential energy density of a scalar field, $\phi $. Red arrows show the classical motion of $\phi $. When $\phi $ is near region (a), the energy density will remain nearly constant, $\rho \approx \rho _{f}$, even as the Universe expands. Moreover, cosmic expansion acts like a frictional drag, slowing the motion of $\phi $: Even near regions (b) and (d), $\phi $ behaves more like a marble moving in a bowl of molasses, slowly creeping down the side of its potential, rather than like a marble sliding down the inside of a polished bowl. During this period of "slow roll," $\rho $ remains nearly constant. Only after $\phi $ has slid most of the way down its potential will it begin to oscillate around its minimum, as in region (c), ending inflation. Credits: \href  {https://ned.ipac.caltech.edu/level5/March05/Guth/Guth1.html}{this website}.\relax }}{42}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\mph@setcol{ii:42}{\mph@nr}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Example of a slow-roll potential as postulated by Linde, Albrecht, and Steinhardt. Inflation occurs in the shaded parts of the potential. Credits: \cite  {Baumann_2022}.\relax }}{43}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fgr:slowroll}{{7}{43}{Example of a slow-roll potential as postulated by Linde, Albrecht, and Steinhardt. Inflation occurs in the shaded parts of the potential. Credits: \cite {Baumann_2022}.\relax }{figure.caption.13}{}}
